# The shift is in "desire more". In S1, S39 and S42 it is the unjust man's grasping for gain over others.
# In the arts passage (S17-S29) it is outdoing others in the practice of a skill, as in tightening strings or prescribing.
# A2 carries the pattern found for the skilled over to the just and unjust, read in the sense of gain.
just	one
unjust	one
knowing	one
ignorant	one
wise	one
good	one
unlike_only	two	more	P3 C1	seeks advantage or gain over the unlike but not over the like	seeks to exceed the unlike, but not the like, in the practice of a skill
both	two	more	P4 C2	seeks advantage or gain over everyone, like and unlike	seeks to exceed everyone, like and unlike, in the practice of a skill
like_wise	one
like_ignorant	one
bridge	A2	more	exceeding others in the practice of a skill	seeking advantage or gain over others
